    pg_ch('1. Architecture, Types & DDL Mastery', 'PostgreSQL Data Types: TEXT vs VARCHAR, TIMESTAMPTZ, UUID, NUMERIC & Arrays', 'postgresql data types, text vs varchar postgres, timestamptz vs timestamp, gen_random_uuid, numeric money',
        'PostgreSQL\'s type system is famously rich—and several "common SQL myths" from other databases are completely different in Postgres!',
        ['**Postgres Fact #1 (`TEXT` vs `VARCHAR(n)`)**: Internally in PostgreSQL (`varlena`), `TEXT`, `VARCHAR`, and `VARCHAR(255)` use the **exact same storage layout and speed**! Prefer `TEXT` (with a `CHECK (length(col) <= 255)` constraint if length validation is desired, because altering a `CHECK` constraint doesn\'t require rewriting metadata locks like `VARCHAR` changes once did).', '**Postgres Fact #2 (Always Use `TIMESTAMPTZ`, Never `TIMESTAMP`)**: Both occupy **8 bytes**, storing UTC microseconds! `TIMESTAMPTZ` converts inputs/outputs cleanly across session timezones, whereas `TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE` silently discards timezone offsets!', '**Native `UUID` (`16 bytes`)**: `gen_random_uuid()` is built-in since PG 13 (`uuidv7()` in PG 17/18!) and occupies **16 binary bytes**, vs **36 bytes** if stored clumsily as `VARCHAR(36)`!'],
        [['`TEXT`', 'Variable-length UTF-8 string (up to 1 GB via TOAST)', 'Identical performance to `VARCHAR(n)` in PostgreSQL!'], ['`TIMESTAMPTZ`', '8-byte UTC timestamp with timezone awareness', 'Official PostgreSQL Wiki rule: "Don\'t use `timestamp` (without time zone)."'], ['`NUMERIC(12, 2)`', 'Arbitrary-precision exact decimal', 'Never use Postgres\'s legacy locale-dependent `MONEY` type—always use `NUMERIC` + currency code!'], ['`UUID DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()`', '16-byte binary UUID', '55% smaller index footprint than `CHAR(36)`.']],
        "SELECT GEN_RANDOM_UUID() AS order_uuid, NOW() AS utc_timestamp;",
